# Copy to docker-compose.override.yml (git-ignored; ./setup.sh --host-network does it) to let the Sentinel scan this machine's LAN directly
# instead of only what the Docker bridge network reaches. docker compose reads the override file on its own, and because it is not tracked the
# updater can still fast-forward the checkout. nmap's OS detection, naabu's SYN scan and the UDP sweep keep using the one capability
# docker-compose.yml grants (NET_RAW, with every other capability dropped, NET_ADMIN included), so host networking needs no privileged mode and the
# set is the same as on the bridge network. With it, loopback and every address of this machine belong to the Sentinel's network too.
#
# What is still exposed with host networking: the container shares the host's network namespace, so a tool compromised by hostile scan output can
# still open raw and packet sockets there (NET_RAW), which means it can sniff the traffic that reaches the host's interfaces and send forged packets
# from them, and it can connect to every service that listens on the host's loopback or LAN addresses (set CVENGINE_SENTINEL_NETWORK and
# CVENGINE_SENTINEL_DENY_RANGES to keep the scanner itself away from them). It cannot change the host's routes, firewall or interfaces (that needs
# NET_ADMIN, which is dropped), and the root filesystem is read-only.
services:
  sentinel:
    network_mode: host
